  "id": "DrugBank_DB00846 - MONDO_4980",
  "verbalization": "Flurandrenolide appears to be a potential repurposed drug for atopic eczema, as it is linked through multiple indirect pathways to the condition. The evidence suggests that Flurandrenolide is associated with lichen disease, a broad category of skin conditions, and that lichen disease is linked to specific corticosteroids such as prednicarbate, flumethasone, and amcinonide. These corticosteroids are known to be effective in treating atopic eczema by reducing inflammation and immune-mediated responses. The cumulative evidence from these paths indicates that Flurandrenolide may exert a therapeutic effect on atopic eczema through a mechanism involving the modulation of inflammatory pathways, likely through the use of corticosteroids as a downstream component of its therapeutic action. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of corticosteroids in the treatment of atopic eczema, with Flurandrenolide potentially serving as a precursor or indirect agent in this process.",
